    "openrouter_cost_usd": 0.009831,
    "reply": "Genetic association records link Cystic fibrosis to several genes [2]. The associated entities comprise Gene NCBIGene:1080, name: CF transmembrane conductance regulator [1]. Additional identified loci include Gene NCBIGene:2212, name: Fc gamma receptor IIa [3]. The condition is also linked to Gene NCBIGene:7040, name: transforming growth factor beta 1 [4].\n\n## Associated Genes\n\nThe primary locus linked to the disorder is Gene NCBIGene:1080, name: CF transmembrane conductance regulator [1]. Modifier and associated loci include Gene NCBIGene:2212, name: Fc gamma receptor IIa [3]. Furthermore, disease association is established for Gene NCBIGene:7040, name: transforming growth factor beta 1 [4].\n\n## Clinical Manifestations\n\nPatients with Cystic fibrosis present with pulmonary and systemic findings [2]. Clinical features documented in medical genetics records include Bronchiectasis [15]. Documented manifestations also encompass Clubbing of fingers [19]. Gastrointestinal and metabolic signs include Dehydration [23].\n\n## Hepatic Complications\n\nHepatic involvement represents a recognized manifestation, with features including Hepatomegaly [29]. While liver disease is common, brain dysfunction remains an infrequent presentation [26: \"however encephalopathy is a rare complication\"]. Stable liver disease can develop high ammonia levels following exposure to systemic corticosteroids [26: \"hyperammonemic encephalopathy\\\" can be precipitated in previously stable CFLD by various triggers including systemic corticosteroids\"].",
